WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:11.440
Hello, my name is Ed Prior, and I'm a ZOS function tester, and I've come here to talk a little bit more in depth about a few things that Jeff mentioned.

2
00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:19.440
Specifically, we support conditional logic inside JCL. You can use if, then, and else to control the flow of your job.

3
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:26.440
So if you have a program that has a return code associated with it, you can actually code into your JCL,

4
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:35.440
you know, slash slash if return code less than four, or if return code greater than eight, or if return code is greater than four but less than eight,

5
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:43.440
then do this step, else do this other step. It gives you a great deal of control over what your program is actually doing.

6
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:53.440
We also mentioned nested procedures. So if you follow the general rule, don't put the sequence proc proc pend pend in your code,

7
00:00:53.440 --> 00:01:01.440
you won't bump into this issue. The idea being that you're not allowed to code a procedure inside of another procedure.

8
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:08.440
They have to be separate. So proc pend, then proc pend, not proc proc pend. Pretty cool, right?

9
00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:17.440
While you're working with procedures, you can nest them, as we mentioned, but you also are allowed to scope symbols as you drop down that chain.

10
00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:26.440
So as you dive deeper into your nested procs, you end up allowing yourself to overwrite different symbols at different levels. Pretty cool.

11
00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:35.440
One thing I learned recently was the actual connecting fiber between those DD statements in your JCL and the actual programs you're running.

12
00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:45.440
So the names on those DDs, so your sys in or your sort in or your out whatever, that's actually controlled by the programmer who wrote the program.

13
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:54.440
So you give it the DD name that's controlled by whatever they opened as a DCB inside their program.

14
00:01:54.440 --> 00:02:03.440
Talking more about dispositions in JCL, if you want to have a temporary data set that you can stuff output into and then forget about later,

15
00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:14.440
you can use the disposition combination new delete on your DD statement to specify a data set that the system will clean up at job termination time,

16
00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:22.440
allowing you to fill the needs that your program has without having to worry about allocating space on a disk beforehand.

17
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:28.440
I'm Ed Prior, and this has been just a deeper dive into a few topics. I hope you've enjoyed.
